## 2️⃣ Detailed migration steps

### 2.1 Install the new packages

```bash
# Keep the draw package (it works as-is)
npm install @mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw   # already in your project – no change

# Replace the geocoder with the MapLibre fork
npm uninstall @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

> **Tip:** If you prefer a fully-native draw experience, you can also install Terra Draw (see the “Alternative” column above). The API is identical, only the CSS import changes.

---

### 2.2 Update imports & CSS order

```javascript
/* -------------------------------------------------
   MAPLIBRE CORE (you already did this in step 1)
   ------------------------------------------------- */
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

/* -------------------------------------------------
   DRAW (unchanged import, but CSS class rename)
   ------------------------------------------------- */
import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css';   // <-- keep this

/* -------------------------------------------------
   GEOCODER (new import, new CSS)
   ------------------------------------------------- */
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';
```

**Why the CSS order matters**  
Both draw and geocoder ship their own CSS that references the `mapboxgl-` prefix. By loading the MapLibre core CSS **first**, the later imports will override the prefix-specific rules with the MapLibre equivalents (the forked packages internally replace the selectors at runtime). If you notice UI glitches, you can also add a tiny manual override:

```css
/* In your own stylesheet (after the imports) */
.mapboxgl-draw-button { /* old selector */
  /* mapbox->maplibre rename */
}
.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder { /* old selector */ }
```

But the forked geocoder already does the rename for you; the draw CSS only needs the class-name change described next.

---

### 2.3 Rename any **custom** CSS that targets `mapboxgl-` classes

Search your codebase for the string `mapboxgl-` and replace it with `maplibregl-`. Typical places:

| Old selector | New selector |
|--------------|--------------|
| `.mapboxgl-ctrl` | `.maplibregl-ctrl` |
| `.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder` | `.maplibregl-ctrl-geocoder` |
| `.mapboxgl-draw-button` | `.maplibregl-draw-button` |
| `.mapboxgl-draw-point` | `.maplibregl-draw-point` |
| `.mapboxgl-draw-polygon` | `.maplibregl-draw-polygon` |
| … | … |

If you have **no custom CSS** (you rely only on the library-provided styles) you can skip this step.

---

### 2.4 Initialise the **draw** control (unchanged API)

```javascript
// Create the map (you already did this)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // or your own style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

// Initialise draw – exactly the same as before
const draw = new MapboxDraw({
  displayControlsDefault: false,
  controls: {
    polygon: true,
    line_string: true,
    point: true,
    trash: true
  }
});
map.addControl(draw);
```

**What changed?** Nothing in the JavaScript API. The only thing you might notice is that the default CSS class names on the toolbar now start with `maplibregl-`. If you used `draw.getMode()` or `draw.changeMode()` you can keep those calls unchanged.

**Optional – Terra Draw** (if you want a MapLibre-specific CSS file)

```bash
npm i terra-draw terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter
```

```javascript
import { TerraDrawMapLibreGLAdapter } from 'terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter';
import { TerraDraw } from 'terra-draw';

const adapter = new TerraDrawMapLibreGLAdapter({ map });
const terraDraw = new TerraDraw(adapter, {
  modes: [TerraDrawPolygonMode, TerraDrawLineStringMode, TerraDrawPointMode, TerraDrawSelectMode, TerraDrawDeleteMode]
});
terraDraw.start();
```

The API is a superset of `mapbox-gl-draw`; you can keep your existing draw-related code and just swap the import if you prefer.

---

### 2.5 Initialise the **geocoder** control (new import + service endpoint)

#### 2.5.1 Choose a geocoding service

| Service | Endpoint (example) | Notes |
|---------|-------------------|-------|
| **Nominatim (OpenStreetMap)** | `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1&q={query}` | Free for low-volume testing. Requires proper OSM attribution. |
| **Photon (Komoot)** | `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q={query}` | Fast, lightweight. |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | Requires a MapTiler API key (free tier available). |
| **OpenRouteService** (also provides reverse) | `https://api.openrouteservice.org/geocode/search?api_key=YOUR_KEY&text={query}` | Requires API key. |
| **Custom/self-hosted** | `https://my-geocoder.example.com/search?q={query}` | Any GeoJSON-compatible service works. |

Pick one that matches your usage and budget. For the purpose of this guide we’ll use **Nominatim** (no key required).

#### 2.5.2 Create the geocoder instance

```javascript
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';

// Nominatim – no API key needed
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // The `placeholder` and `flyTo` options work exactly as in the Mapbox version
  placeholder: 'Search places …',
  maplibregl: maplibregl,          // required for the internal `flyTo` helper
  // Tell the geocoder which endpoint to hit
  // The library expects a function that returns a Promise of GeoJSON
  // (the default implementation works for Mapbox; we replace it)
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const response = await fetch(
      `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`
    );
    const data = await response.json();
    // Nominatim returns a FeatureCollection already – just return it
    return {
      features: data.features
    };
  },
  // Optional: limit results, bias to a viewport, etc.
  // See the README of @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder for all options
});

// Add the control to the map (same position as before)
map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');
```

**If you already have a Mapbox token in the old geocoder options** – simply delete the `accessToken` property; the MapLibre fork will ignore it.

#### 2.5.3 CSS class rename (already covered)

The geocoder CSS shipped by `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` already uses the `maplibregl-` prefix, so you only need to import the stylesheet (see step 2.2). If you had any custom overrides that referenced `.mapboxgl-ctrl-geocoder`, rename them to `.maplibregl-ctrl-geocoder`.

