## 3️⃣ Full example (all pieces together)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html

**What’s happening**

`map.loadImage` changed in **MapLibre GL JS v4** (and later).  
- In Mapbox GL JS (and in MapLibre v1-v3) the method accepted a *callback*:

```js
map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => { … })
```

- Starting with **MapLibre v4.0.0** the callback signature was **removed** and the method now **returns a Promise** that resolves with an object `{ data: ImageBitmap }`.  
Because you are using the *latest* `maplibre-gl` (v6 at the time of writing), the callback you pass is simply ignored, so the image never gets added and any markers that rely on that image disappear.

---

## 1️⃣ Fix the `loadImage` call

### Option A – Promise syntax

```js
// inside a map-load handler or any async context
map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
  .then(({ data }) => {
    // `data` is an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement in older browsers)
    map.addImage('pin', data);
  })
  .catch(err => {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  });
```

### Option B – async/await (cleaner if you can use `async`)

```js
async function addPinImage() {
  try {
    const { data } = await map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png');
    map.addImage('pin', data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  }
}

// Call it after the map is ready
map.on('load', addPinImage);
```

> **Tip:** If you already have an `async` function that creates the map, you can `await` the `loadImage` right after the `new maplibregl.Map(...)` line.

---

## 2️⃣ Make sure the image is added **after** the map’s style is loaded

`map.addImage` can only be called once the style’s sprite has been parsed (i.e. after the `load` event). If you try to add the image earlier you’ll get a silent failure.

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // safe place to call loadImage / addImage
  addPinImage();
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ Why the marker icons disappear

There are two common reasons:

| Reason | What to check / fix |
|--------|----------------------|
| **You were using the default Mapbox “pin” sprite** (`mapbox://sprites/...`) | MapLibre does **not** understand `mapbox://` sprite URLs. Replace the style’s `sprite` property with a URL that points to a sprite you host (or use a public one, e.g. the OpenFreeMap style). If you only need a single custom icon, the `loadImage → addImage` approach above is enough. |
| **Your style still references a `sprite` that contains the “pin” image** | Open the style JSON (or use Maputnik) and verify the `sprite` URL is reachable and that the sprite sheet actually contains an entry named `"pin"`. If you switched to a completely different style (e.g. `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty`), that style’s sprite will not have a `"pin"` entry, so you must add your own image as shown above. |

---

## 4️⃣ Full minimal example (MapLibre v6)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom marker</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  map.on('load', async () => {
    try {
      const { data } = await map.loadImage(
        'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/custom-marker.png'
      );
      map.addImage('pin', data);

      // Add a source + layer that uses the image
      map.addSource('point', {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: {
          type: 'FeatureCollection',
          features: [{
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] },
            properties: {}
          }]
        }
      });

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'point-layer',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'point',
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'pin',
          'icon-size': 0.5,
          'icon-allow-overlap': true
        }
      });
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('❌ image load failed', e);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Run this snippet (or copy it into a local file) and you’ll see a custom pin marker appear.*

---

## 5️⃣ Quick checklist after the fix

1. **`map.loadImage` → promise** (no callback).  
2. **Call it after `map.on('load')`** (or after the style is ready).  
3. **Verify the style’s `sprite` URL** does not point to a `mapbox://` resource.  
4. **If you rely on a default “pin” sprite**, either:  
   - host your own sprite sheet and reference it in the style, **or**  
   - load a single image with `loadImage`/`addImage` as shown above.  
5. **Check the console** for any CORS or 404 errors on the image URL – those will also prevent the image from being added.

---

### TL;DR

```js
// Replace this (callback – no longer works)
map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => { … });

/* With this (promise) */
map.loadImage(url)
   .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
   .catch(err => console.error(err));
```

After you make that change (and ensure the style isn’t still trying to pull a `mapbox://` sprite), your custom marker icons will re-appear. 🎉

If you run into any other Mapbox-specific URLs (e.g., `mapbox://fonts/…` or `mapbox://tiles/…`) or plugins that still reference `mapboxgl`, let me know and we can walk through those migrations as well.

### Quick fixes for the two things you mentioned  

| What you have in Mapbox GL JS v2 | What you need to do for the **latest maplibre-gl** |
|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| ```js<br>new mapboxgl.Map({<br>  container: 'map',<br>  style: '…',<br>  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,<br>  antialias: true,<br>  …<br>});``` | `preserveDrawingBuffer` and `antialias` are no longer top-level options. They must be placed inside a `canvasContextAttributes` object. |
| ```js<br>map.on('load', a).on('click', b);``` | `map.on()` now returns a **subscription object**, not the map itself, so the chain breaks. Call `on` separately for each event. |

