## 1️⃣ Move the canvas flags into `canvasContextAttributes`

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',   // <-- any non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,

  // ← NEW location for the flags
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,   // keep the canvas after a screenshot, etc.
    antialias: true                // smoother lines / polygons
  },

  // other options you already had (e.g. maxZoom, minZoom, etc.)
});
```

*Why?*  
Starting with **maplibre-gl v5.0.0** the two flags were moved under `canvasContextAttributes` (see the API docs). If you leave them at the top level they are silently ignored, which can cause missing screenshots or jagged edges.

---

## 2️⃣ Un-chain the event listeners

```js
// ❌ This no longer works – `on` returns a subscription, not the map
// map.on('load', a).on('click', b);

// ✅ Call `on` separately (or store the subscription if you need to remove it later)
map.on('load', a);
map.on('click', b);

// If you need to later remove a listener:
const loadSub = map.on('load', a);   // returns a subscription object
loadSub.unsubscribe();              // removes the listener
```

*Why?*  
From **maplibre-gl v5.0.0** onward `Map.on()` returns a **subscription** (`{ unsubscribe(): void }`). The old “fluent” style (`map.on(...).on(...)`) was removed, so each call must be made on the `map` instance itself.

---

## 3️⃣ (Optional) Verify you’re not using any other v2-only APIs

Since you’re already on a v2 Mapbox map, double-check for these common culprits that will throw “is not a function” in MapLibre:

| Mapbox v2 method | MapLibre equivalent (or “not supported”) |
|------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `map.setFog(...)` | `map.setSky(...)` (use the `sky` style property) |
| `map.getFreeCameraOptions()` / `map.setFreeCameraOptions()` | Use `map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation(...)` and then `map.jumpTo/easeTo/flyTo` |
| `map.loadImage(url, cb)` | Returns a **Promise**: `const { data } = await map.loadImage(url)` |
| `source.getClusterExpansionZoom(...)` (callback) | Returns a **Promise** in MapLibre v4+ |

If any of those appear in your code, convert them now (or remove them) to avoid runtime errors.

---

## 4️⃣ Full minimal example (combining the fixes)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

function onLoad() {
  console.log('map loaded');
}
function onClick(e) {
  console.log('clicked at', e.lngLat);
}

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
    antialias: true
  }
});

map.on('load', onLoad);
map.on('click', onClick);
```

Run this and you should see the map render with the requested canvas flags, and both listeners fire correctly.

---

### TL;DR checklist for your case

- **[ ]** Move `preserveDrawingBuffer` & `antialias` into `canvasContextAttributes`.
- **[ ]** Replace any chained `map.on(...).on(...)` with separate `map.on(...)` calls (or store the returned subscription if you need to unsubscribe later).
- **[ ]** Scan the rest of the code for other Mapbox-v2-only APIs (`setFog`, free-camera methods, callback-style async calls) and replace them with the MapLibre equivalents listed above.

Once those two changes are in place, the rest of your map should work exactly as it did under Mapbox GL JS v2. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a quick-reference guide that covers **all the practical ways you can add a search-box (geocoder) to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map**, from the official Mapbox plugin to fully open-source alternatives you can use if you ever migrate to MapLibre or want to avoid the Mapbox token.

---

## 1️⃣ The “official” Mapbox way – `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`

| What it is | When to use it | How to add it | Key options you’ll need |
|------------|----------------|---------------|--------------------------|
| A ready-made UI control that talks to the **Mapbox Geocoding API** (forward-geocode, reverse-geocode, autocomplete). | You already have a Mapbox access token and are happy to use Mapbox’s hosted geocoder (free tier ≤ 100 k requests / month, paid plans above). | 1. Install the package  <br>```bash npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder``` <br>2. Import the CSS and JS  <br>```js import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';``` <br>```js import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';``` <br>3. Create the control and add it to the map. | *accessToken* (your Mapbox token) <br>*placeholder* (e.g. “Search places”) <br>*proximity* (optional – bias results toward the current view) <br>*bbox* (optional – limit results to a bounding box) <br>*language*, *countries*, *types*, *limit* etc. |

### Minimal example (Mapbox GL JS v2)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Mapbox GL + Geocoder</title>
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder@5.0.2/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder@5.0.2/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
    accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
    mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // required to use the built-in UI
    placeholder: 'Search for places',
    proximity: { longitude: -122.42, latitude: 37.78 }, // optional bias
    countries: 'us',             // optional filter
    limit: 5
  });

  // Add the control to the top-left corner
  map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');

  // Optional: react to a result
  geocoder.on('result', e => {
    console.log('Selected place:', e.result);
    // e.result.geometry.coordinates is [lon, lat]
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Things to remember**

* **Token** – The geocoder **must** receive a valid Mapbox access token (the same one you use for the map). If you rotate or revoke the token, the control stops working.
* **Rate limits** – Free tier: 100 k forward-geocode requests per month. If you exceed that, you’ll get HTTP 429 responses.
* **Attribution** – The control automatically adds the required “© Mapbox, © OpenStreetMap” attribution line. Keep it visible.
* **Styling** – The control’s CSS uses the `mapboxgl-` prefix. If you later switch to MapLibre you’ll need to rename those classes (or use the MapLibre-specific fork – see §2).

---

## 2️⃣ “MapLibre-compatible” version – `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`

If you ever plan to **migrate to MapLibre** (or you already are using it), the same UI can be used with a **different backend** (any service that follows the Mapbox Geocoding API schema). The package is a thin fork that swaps the `mapboxgl-` CSS prefix to `maplibregl-` and removes the hard-coded token requirement.

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No accessToken needed unless you point to Mapbox’s API
  // If you want to keep using Mapbox’s service, just pass the token:
  // accessToken: 'pk.ey...'
  placeholder: 'Search…',
  // optional: use a different endpoint
  // url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY'
});
map.addControl(geocoder);
```

*You can keep the same UI while swapping the backend to any of the open services listed in §3.*

---

## 3️⃣ Open-source / self-hosted geocoder back-ends (no Mapbox token)

| Service | Hosted demo? | License / cost | Typical usage pattern | Example endpoint for the UI |
|---------|--------------|----------------|----------------------|-----------------------------|
| **Nominatim** (OSM) | `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/` (public demo, **rate-limited, not for production**) | OSM data – free, attribution required | Forward-geocode: `GET /search?format=json&q={query}&addressdetails=1&limit=5` | `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&q={query}&limit=5` |
| **Photon** (Komoot) | `https://photon.komoot.io/` (public demo) | Apache 2.0 – free, attribution required | `GET /api/?q={query}&limit=5` | `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q={query}&limit=5` |
| **Pelias** | Public demo at `https://pelias.mapzen.com/` (now deprecated) – better to self-host | BSD-3 – free, attribution required | `GET /v1/search?text={query}&size=5` | `https://pelias.example.com/v1/search?text={query}&size=5` |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | Commercial SaaS, free tier 100 k/mo | Same request shape as Mapbox (JSON schema compatible) | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY` |
| **OpenCage** | `https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?q={query}&key=YOUR_KEY` | Commercial, generous free tier | Slightly different response shape – you’ll need a tiny adapter | `https://api.opencagedata.com/geocode/v1/json?q={query}&key=YOUR_KEY` |

### How to wire an open service to the UI

All of the UI libraries above (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`, `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`) accept a **`url`** option that points to a *Mapbox-compatible* endpoint. Most open services already expose a compatible endpoint (Nominatim, Photon, MapTiler). If the response shape differs (e.g., OpenCage), you can either:

1. **Write a tiny wrapper** that proxies the request and translates the JSON to Mapbox’s schema, **or**
2. **Use the built-in `fetch`** and handle the result yourself (skip the UI library and build a custom dropdown).

#### Example: Nominatim + MapLibre Geocoder

```js
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  placeholder: 'Search OSM places',
  // Nominatim returns GeoJSON-compatible features if you ask for `format=jsonv2`
  url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1&limit=5',
  // Nominatim does not need a token, but you must add the required attribution:
  // (the control will automatically add it if you set `attribution` below)
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
